#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = ["bibtexparser==2.1.0", "pybtex==0.26.1", "pyyaml"]
# ///
"""tools/bib-merge — merge the series' bibliographies into the pool's one file.

Every series keeps its own ``lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib``. The pool has
one: ``lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib``, generated by this tool from the
series' copies in ``mirror/`` and the rulings in ``sync/bib.yml``, and never
edited by hand. It is regenerated after every sync, as the pool lectures are
refreshed, so a key a series adds reaches the pool the next morning.

How a key is merged
-------------------
Each series' copy of an entry is read and NORMALISED first, then the copies
are compared. Normalising applies the house style's mechanical rules
(QuantEcon manual, "Writing a bibliography entry") and nothing else:

* field names and the entry type in lower case;
* whitespace runs collapsed to one space (one field per line);
* ``month`` and the fields a reader never sees (``abstract``, ``keywords``,
  ``file``, ``date-added``, ``date-modified``, ``issn``) dropped, and any
  empty field;
* page ranges written with a double hyphen (``1 - 68``, ``225–287`` and
  ``S1-S20`` become ``1--68``, ``225--287`` and ``S1--S20``);
* a DOI written as a URL reduced to the bare DOI.

Values are otherwise kept byte for byte: no LaTeX is decoded or encoded, no
title re-cased, no name reformatted. A key held by one series is taken as it
is. A key held by several series whose normalised copies are identical is
taken once. A key whose copies differ is a CONFLICT, and needs a ruling in
``sync/bib.yml``:

    rulings:
      Clarke_71:
        take: intermediate          # the copy the result starts from
        set: {pages: 17--33}        # fields to set (optional)
        drop: [volume]              # fields to drop (optional)
        type: book                  # entry type to set (optional)
        because: the publisher's record; owner's ruling of 2026-11-02 (#10)
        seal:                       # each series' copy when the ruling was made
          intermediate: 0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d
          intro: 8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f

The SEAL is the digest of each series' normalised copy at ruling time. A
ruling holds while every copy is either its sealed version or already equal
to the result; a copy edited to anything else REOPENS the ruling, so a change
upstream is never folded in silently. A ruling may also fix a key held by one
series (a style fix the normalising cannot make). ``--propose`` prints a stub,
seal included, for every conflict without a ruling.

What it refuses
---------------
Anything it cannot read exactly, rather than guess: a syntax error, a
duplicate key or field within one file, ``@string`` or ``@preamble``, a bare
macro other than a month, a ``#`` concatenation. A refused FILE stops the
run (exit 2) and nothing is written. A refused KEY (a conflict without a
ruling, or a reopened ruling) does not hold the others back: it keeps the
entry the committed file already has, if any, and the run exits 2 with the
key and the reason.

Checks
------
* citation closure: every key a pool lecture cites resolves in the merged
  file (an error); keys no lecture of any series cites are listed (info);
* style: the non-mechanical rules the normalising cannot apply — the fields
  each entry type needs, names joined by ``and``, keys unique when case is
  ignored (errors); LaTeX escapes (rule 11) and unknown fields (info);
* equivalence: pybtex, the reader Jupyter Book 1 uses, reads every merged
  entry as it reads the series copy the entry came from, apart from the
  normalising and the entry's ruling (an error otherwise). bibtexparser only
  splits the files; pybtex is an independent reader of both sides.

Usage
-----
    tools/bib-merge                  # check: regenerate in memory, compare with
                                     # the committed file, run the checks
    tools/bib-merge --write          # write lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib
    tools/bib-merge --report [PATH]  # the conflicts, side by side (markdown)
    tools/bib-merge --propose        # sync/bib.yml stubs for unsettled conflicts
    tools/bib-merge --reconstruct    # rebuild mirror/ for every series first

Exit status: 0 clean (info only); 1 the committed file is behind its sources
(the series' files moved: run --write); 2 a refused file or key, a failed
check, or a committed file that differs from what the same inputs generate
(a hand edit, or a ruling or tool change that was not regenerated). Under
GitHub Actions the outcome is also written to ``$GITHUB_OUTPUT``:
``bib_state`` (clean, refresh, violation, missing or error), ``bib_failed``
and ``bib_refresh`` (true or false).
"""

